{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse","id":1555708,"title":"Designing a \"Chronicle\" to introduce new players into Werewolf: the Apocalypse","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-20T22:46:57-08:00","id":1555708,"title":"Designing a \"Chronicle\" to introduce new players into Werewolf: the Apocalypse","content":"<u><b><span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><span style=\"font-size:22px\">Before I begin, Disclaimer:</span></span></b></u><br /> <br /> <br /> \u0336D\u0336o\u0336n\u0336'\u0336t\u0336 \u0336t\u0336h\u0336i\u0336n\u0336k\u0336 \u0336&quot;\u0336C\u0336h\u0336r\u0336o\u0336n\u0336i\u0336c\u0336l\u0336e\u0336&quot;\u0336 \u0336i\u0336n\u0336 \u0336t\u0336h\u0336e\u0336 \u0336s\u0336e\u0336n\u0336s\u0336e\u0336 \u0336t\u0336h\u0336a\u0336t\u0336 \u0336y\u0336o\u0336u\u0336 \u0336k\u0336n\u0336o\u0336w\u0336 \u0336i\u0336t\u0336.\u0336 Instead, think more like a series of stories (player-focused in this case), where the players are all playing Homid-breed werewolves who have just had their first change, are together as a pack. They're not Garou YET. They don't have a tribe YET. They don't know what the Umbra is YET.<br /> <br /> BUT! They do however have each other<br /> <br /> That's the sale's pitch.<br /> <br /> Why would I (or anyone) want to make a chronicle like this? Simple:<br /> <br /> To reflect the players, who are all newbies, getting into the game for the very first time, and have LITERALLY NO IDEA what they're getting into, and for Storytellers who want to see peoples minds be completely blown and melted.<br /> <i>&quot;Oh, it's just a game where I run around as a murder-furry! How complex or deep could it possibly be?&quot;<br /> - A player who has yet to see the wondrous beauty of The Umbra.</i><br /> <br /> Goal: For the Storyteller to teach the intricacies and mechanics of the setting to the players, and have the players learn stuff like &quot;The Umbra&quot;, &quot;The Triat&quot;, and &quot;The Garou Nation&quot; through the eyes of their characters.<br /> <br /> Obviously, the players would still know how to play on a MECHANICAL level (Attribute + Ability), and learn more as they play, but overall, they, like their characters, don't know a lot.<br /> <br /> Basically, kinda like Hunter: the Reckoning, but with the framework of Werewolf: the Apocalypse, and roleplaying those &quot;First days of being a werewolf&quot; of those characters.<br /> <br /> The Climax of the chronicle would be encountering the Garou Nation - or maybe the PCs learning their first spirit Gift. I dunno.<br /> <br /> Rule 1: MAXIMUM PLAYER BAMBOOZLE IN A WAY THAT'S FUN AND REWARDING.<br /> That's what I'm going for.<br /> <br /> This chronicle is meant to be designed for Storytellers who want their players going &quot;Wait a minute, WE CAN DO THAT?!&quot; Over and over again as they embrace the game. It'll be great.<br /> <br /> I had a two-page character sheet that was hand-crafted for this, but I lost it when my external hard drive crashed, so that sucks, and I'd need to rebuild it completely from scratch, and I don't want to do that! I have been using a homebrewed version of the Ronin rules, but it doesn't feel exactly right when your players are not just uninitiated cubs, but also people who haven't played a WoD game outside of maybe Vampire: the Masquerade and maybe the Bloodlines game.<br /> <br /> Anyways, if anyone wants to pitch in with their thoughts, two cents, or (in my opinion, extremely important because I can never seem to find them) the books that storytellers should take another browse through if they want to do this, then I'd highly appreciate it! Thanks in advance!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":". 11-22-2025, 03:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T00:31:48-08:00","id":1555711,"title":"","content":"With a broad stoke think about it like the Vampire played from the last few mortla days to experience the Embrace and the first nights to be a fledgeling<br /> Similarly, the wouldbe Werewolves strat as cubs before their Firts Change (or maybe Lost Cubs, maybe would work better, because a Garou kid is usually tutored by on the theoretical level by the local sept/packs asigned to d othis, Kinfolks, etc)<br /> A few last days then First Change triggered by anything really and then comes the Cub bootcap untill the rite of passage","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T00:39:55-08:00","id":1555712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555711#post1555711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With a broad stoke think about it like the Vampire played from the last few mortla days to experience the Embrace and the first nights to be a fledgeling<br /> Similarly, the wouldbe Werewolves strat as cubs before their Firts Change (or maybe Lost Cubs, maybe would work better, because a Garou kid is usually tutored by on the theoretical level by the local sept/packs asigned to d othis, Kinfolks, etc)<br /> A few last days then First Change triggered by anything really and then comes the Cub bootcap untill the rite of passage</div> </div> </div> </div>Werewolf preludes probably have more in common with Imbued preludes than Vampire ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T05:49:31-08:00","id":1555715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse#post1555708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't think &quot;Chronicle&quot; in the sense that you know it. Instead, think more like a series of stories (player-focused in this case)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the definition of a Chronicle, it doesn't have to be about regular starting characters by the books.<br /> <br /> Also, that's one of the first ideas most new STs have for a game.<br /> <br /> It isn't a bad idea, but pulling it out is harder than it seems at first, so we need something more specific and elaborated than this sales pitch.<br /> <br /> What's the story? Having a sandbox for the PCs to explore while they learn the rules is nice and dandy, but without an actual story to follow it'll bog down quickly, because they don't even know how or why to try things. The biggest challenge is to design a plot that will work as tutorial and still be fun to play.<br /> <br /> Giving the right amount of info beforehand is also important, because players have their own expectations and may not match your enthusiasm when they learn the game isn't about what they thought it would be. They may also find it frustrating to be on the dark about everything they can do or they're facing.<br /> <br /> Your final challenge is to deal with actual player knowledge and understanding. Some may have a harder time getting what's going on than you expect, and that can mess with the game. But also, many will get it easier than we expect. How complex or deep could it possibly be? We live in a world post-WoD and post-<i>Underworld</i>, the bad movie that nonetheless was a success and made WoD-style Werewolves the Hollywood standard for the next two decades at least. Be ready for a lot of players getting things way before you expect.<br /> <br /> Finally, you have to deal with both players that pretend to not know the game, but actually know it, and players that do research about it between sessions and may end up knowing even more than you do. Not easy to deal with that unless you already know the group very well.<br /> <br /> So, as interesting as the pitch is, and it really is, I wish to see a more detailed plan than just &quot;that would be cool&quot;. It's hard, been there, done that, and seen many others do it too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T06:43:24-08:00","id":1555722,"title":"","content":"While I tend to be much more adamant about not doing this in other WoD games (Mage the most), there is an axiom I hold to regarding this idea:<br /> <br /> You can't learn without doing. The problem with starting with characters significantly below the WoD games starting point is that this frequently means the players never directly learn things in-play because they don't have the ability to do those things yet. Which leads to what monteparnas was saying regarding some players just reading to figure it out, and others just feeling lost and listless.<br /> <br /> WtA also has a lot of tools for doing the sort of &quot;as everyone should already know&quot; exposition dumps in how Garou culture was designed. A Sept's monthly Moot traditionally includes:<br /> <br /> - Recitation of the Litany with Ragabash hecklers to add color and nuance<br /> - More experienced Galliards telling stories from ancient tales that they feel could be relevant to today, to boasting the Sept's latest accomplishments, to mourning the loss of warriors; and it all has to be stuff the freshest Cubs could get<br /> - Watching the more experienced Philodoxes and Theurges running the event, settling social and spiritual matters, and ensuring all the ceremonial stuff is handled<br /> - General announcements of news everyone needs to hear about<br /> - Formal challenges to see how those are handled (intros to the mechanics for them as well, including combat)<br /> - The party after business is over to get some informal social stuff in<br /> - The thing ends with a big ritual hunt too (so more chances for some basic combat)<br /> <br /> A lot of STs IME kinda speed through Moots because... a lot of that is fairly repetitive and gets boring fast for experienced WtA players outside of some juicy plot events that might be the real focus. But they make excellent starting points for new WtA players because it's all about Garou culture, it's designed to get Cubs and fresh Cliaths into the flow of ritual life, and info dumping is literally something that supposed to happen. There's almost no core mechanics to the game that can't come up in a Moot. There's also a lot of options if you want to adjust things. For example if you plan on the PCs operating out of a small low level Caern where the Moots they've been in so far are pretty small things because there's so few Garou around, the game can start with a visit to a major Caern that their Sept needs to keep on good terms with. If you want to start with a big Caern so you can have a big cast of NPCs, you can have visitors coming in to add spice, plot hooks, and reasons for things to get explained in more detail that &quot;normal.&quot; Etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T15:14:16-08:00","id":1555745,"title":"","content":"It's important to consider that the books recognize and state this difficulty directly.<br /> <br /> Your &quot;standard&quot; starting character is expected to have been a full splat member for some time when the game starts, and also expected to have been directly educated about their condition by a senior member or more, precisely so the character would have had the time to pick on everything they need for that starting sheet to work.<br /> <br /> Characters that don't get this education exist, but they're <b>explicitly</b> shown at considerable disadvantage, and one of those is precisely to be ignorant on a lot of basic stuff, including stuff they can do, sometimes for their whole life (which, granted, tend to be short).<br /> <br /> The simplest option for a game in this style is to have an actual guide teaching the PCs who have some reason to not spill all the beans up front.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T14:58:45-08:00","id":1555769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555712#post1555712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Werewolf preludes probably have more in common with Imbued preludes than Vampire ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those are along the lines of what I was thinking. Yea.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a><br /> <br /> <br /> Those are all good points that you brought up and I'm happy that you did so.<br /> <br /> Let's make a list so we can keep track of them:<br /> <br /> 1 - Need <b>something more specific and elaborated</b> than this sales pitch.<br /> <br /> 2 - Without an actual story to follow, it'll bog down quickly, because<b> they won't even know how or why to try things.</b><br /> I'm imagining that the plot(s) of the early stories are going to need to be simple as a lot of this is going to be driven by the players and their characters. I'll keep this in mind.<br /> <br /> <br /> 3 - Design <b>a plot that will work as tutorial and still be fun to play</b> (High priority, as it is highest challenge).<br /> (Note: I'm thinking with the Story structure, it might be several extremely simple mini-plots that SEEM disconnected at first, but then connect over time, depending on things. There's A LOT of cool directions we can go from here. All we need to do is just find the path forward to do that).<br /> <br /> I'm also thinking that the plot(s) of these early-game stories could correlate and play into why the Garou and Garou nation aren't scooping the PCs up immediately, as there could be a more immediate threat that the closest sept needs to deal with RIGHT THIS INSTANT before they go to collect the Player Characters (I mean, the Garou and spirits ain't gonna ignore them - bringing the Player Characters into the fold is literally the end-game).<br /> <br /> <br /> 4 - <b>The right amount of info beforehand.</b> Players may also find it frustrating to be on the dark about everything they can do or they're facing.<br /> (Note: This is where foreshadowing the Umbra will come in extremely handy. There could be a mirror-spirit available with gifts relating to stepping sideways or peeking, might be a good way to encourage players to try things out, as you've pointed out in 1. As stated, there's a bunch of cool directions that can be taken! We just need to find them first. lol)<br /> <br /> <br /> 5 - <b>Deal with actual player knowledge and understanding.</b> How complex or deep could it possibly be?<br /> (Funny you said that, because I said that exact same thing when I picked up Werewolf back in 2013. Rofl)<br /> <br /> I'm operating off of the idea that the players are going to be brand new to the system (and maybe have played V:tM - Bloodlines), but if there are older players who have experience, they get a front row seat to watching people's minds get blown to how deep the rabbit holes go.<br /> <br /> God, this actually takes me back to the very first time I tried to play Werewolf (&quot;play&quot; as in, nobody wanted to run the game - and one of the older players swore to never run a WoD game again, so I had no choice but to run the game, and the entire Chronicle was older players helping me and new players learn the system. It was a hilarious train wreck of shenanigans that I'd gladly do all over again)!<br /> I have had players who have read and played the early editions of Werewolf before, and when I asked if they were comfortable with helping me and the players as both an in-and-out of character experiment, they accepted. As I said earlier, it was the most fun we've had in a TTRPG. The older player I mentioned had such fun that he actually considered running WoD games again.<br /> <br /> <br /> 6 - Finally, you have to deal with both<b> players that pretend to not know the game, but actually know it, and players that do research about it between sessions and may end up knowing even more than you do</b>.<br /> <br /> That is an extremely good and valid concern!<br /> <br /> One solution could be to just have the players' characters match what the players themselves have known at session zero, and establish with the players &quot;Hey, if you're an older player, then I, the ST will grant you a couple dots in Occult so you're not constantly distracted with whether or not your character would or would not know a specific thing. Plus, it's not outside of the realm of possibility that you're who the Garou Nation sent to bring these pups into the fold in the first place. Would you be ok with that as your character's setup?&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Also</b>, asking the newer players &quot;Hey, are you okay with only having as much knowledge of what's going on as your character(s) are, and learning alongside your character as you continue to play?&quot;<br /> Of course, if the player(s) already enjoy playing something like Call of Cthulhu, then I don't see why they wouldn't be ok with this, but it's still common sense to ask the question and check.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Also, to factor in what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> brought up:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't learn without doing. The problem with starting with characters significantly below the WoD games starting point is that this frequently means the players never directly learn things in-play because they don't have the ability to do those things yet. Which leads to what monteparnas was saying regarding some players just reading to figure it out, and others just feeling lost and listless. </div> </div> </div>And as Heavy Arms also said: <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of STs IME kinda speed through Moots because... a lot of that is fairly repetitive and gets boring fast for experienced WtA players outside of some juicy plot events that might be the real focus. But they make excellent starting points for new WtA players because it's all about Garou culture, it's designed to get Cubs and fresh Cliaths into the flow of ritual life, and info dumping is literally something that supposed to happen. There's almost no core mechanics to the game that can't come up in a Moot. </div> </div> </div>I heavily agree with the &quot;can't learn without doing&quot;. It was the biggest reason why my first attempt at running Werewolf was such a train wreck: I literally didn't know about the setting and got side-blinded by The Umbra (which has now become my favorite part about the setting), and all because I never had a chance to PLAY Werewolf before that point.<br /> <br /> If I'm also understanding Heavy Arms correctly, the Moot is something a lot of ST's ignore due to it getting a bit repetitive (but a new ST wouldn't know this - at least not at first).<br /> In this specific chronicle however, the Moot would be the climax of the Chronicle where everything the new players have learned via their characters, now finally starts to make sense, because every mechanic the players have learned and experience was building up to this point.<br /> <br /> I love the IRL irony of that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The simplest option for a game in this style is to have an actual guide teaching the PCs who have some reason to not spill all the beans up front. </div> </div> </div>And that's a gap that an older player could easily fill (and have tons of fun with, depending on what they want to do for the character they want to play). If a veteran player isn't around, then a Spirit guide or an NPC could &quot;politely nudge&quot; the players into exploring the mechanics - but it'd be much more fun with an older player who's already familiar with the Garou's gameplay intricacies.<br /> <br /> <br /> Anyways, this is all a good start! Now, what are some good starting points we can go from here?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T18:30:57-08:00","id":1555777,"title":"","content":"Well, when it comes to new STs Moots can be a lot because it's a lot to learn if you don't already have some familiarity with the game. I don't think WtA is necessarily the best first game to run if you've never done it before. Even with in the WoD games, there are games where the base premise is much closer to pop-culture expectations (VtM is the poster child of the franchise for a reason), and games where their aren't nearly as many small exceptional moving parts in the mechanics to keep in mind.<br /> <br /> That said, I'm highly advocating for a &quot;introducing a bunch of new players to WtA&quot; not start with &quot;a bunch of freshly changed Garou that have no clue&quot; that climaxes with their first Moot. As you seem to agree, that means a lot of learning but outside of the context where doing is a primary consideration. I'm advocating starting with closer to base-line starting PCs and a Moot because it's a lot of doing in a highly controlled event in-universe to create a framework for the players to feel comfortable in while they learn about stuff. <br /> <br /> Of course, I've also run a lot of HtR, and I think people vastly overstate the idea that the Imbued are all clueless and don't know anything. Beyond the numerous low level information gathering powers in the game, Hunter-net is there with lots of information they can easily have access too. What HtR does differently from the rest of the WoD - and frankly most games broadly - is that the Imbued are dealing with a much more modern problem. They have too much information, and they don't know how to sort the information by quality. Trying to find out information is like trying to learn about stuff from Reddit: a thread can easily devolve into dozens of tangents as people argue their pet theories instead of making sure a new hunter has the basic details they need. The survival guide is both a supplement and an in-game text (though not one made like the Book of Nod or Hunter: Apocrypha) specifically because some of the Hnet users wanted to create something to help new Imbued out so until they get more comfortable learning how to navigate Hnet's weird culture. HtR isn't about playing people with no information, it's about playing people with no context for way too much information.<br /> <br /> The sticking point for Hunter - IME - is the Conviction economy, not the amount of information the PCs have access to. That's something I work to address early and quickly because it completely dominates the base mechanics of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-23T18:26:51-08:00","id":1555817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555769#post1555769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2 - Without an actual story to follow, it'll bog down quickly, because<b> they won't even know how or why to try things.</b><br /> I'm imagining that the plot(s) of the early stories are going to need to be simple as a lot of this is going to be driven by the players and their characters. I'll keep this in mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually simple isn't the important part. The important part is to have stuff happening and clear goals to achieve at each step.<br /> <br /> Even a simple plot can be extremely obtuse in such a situation because it depends on doing something the players have no clue they have to try. Think about crossing into the Umbra: what do they have to do to accomplish that? How would they even know they have to accomplish that?<br /> <br /> Putting a mirror in the scene with a vision of the Umbra may work just fine. But if it don't, you may end up with PCs that not only won't try to even touch it, they may avoid or try to break the mirror, they may see the Umbra as a threat and the mirror as a barrier, and nothing else. And this will happen a lot down the line. So they need a clear reason to try to reach that strange place, and a clear goal to achieve there instead of just wander aimlessly.<br /> <br /> Your plot may actually be as complex as you want, as long as its pieces have clear paths to follow, so the game won't drag when they're lost, and some action happening, to keep them engaged and curious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555769#post1555769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3 - Design <b>a plot that will work as tutorial and still be fun to play</b> (High priority, as it is highest challenge).\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>The challenge here is the Tutorial part.<br /> <br /> What I mean by tutorial is how the plot will actively <i><b>teach</b></i> the players about the things they can do and how they do them.<br /> <br /> Basically that's what an instructor is usually meant to do. Eliminating the instructor or limiting their role means you have to make the plot itself do the work. That's a tutorial.<br /> <br /> And this is not a &quot;so we just have to make a tutorial&quot; thing. Actually doing it is hard. You have to jot down <i>everything</i> the players have to learn, and for each and every bit you must have a plot point where the players will both learn that it exist in some form and how to try it, and every single time you run the risk of your players not getting it and roaming around without a purpose.<br /> <br /> Everything else comes after that. If you don't have the tutorial structure, then you don't have a functional plot for a game you'll start by not telling your players what they can do.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555769#post1555769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4 - <b>The right amount of info beforehand.</b> Players may also find it frustrating to be on the dark about everything they can do or they're facing.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>This is not just about foreshadowing, that's the easy part.<br /> <br /> What's hard is to devise an adequate pitch to invite the players.<br /> <br /> Making a game where the players don't actually know what kind of game they're going to play is fraught with traps, because you may find out too late that some of the things you love about the game are actually no-nos for a given player. Will they enjoy playing spiritual warriors? Eco-fighters? Having Auspices? Ripping their clothes to shreds? Being Rage-fueled killer machines that can loose their temperament at any time? Will they like the Garou society when all is said and done? After all, a bad ending may spoil the entire experience.<br /> <br /> You may also find out that they <i>really want</i> things you didn't consider, like playing through the drama of a curse or the idea of tying oneself up in full moon days. There are many werewolf stories out there that aren't just <i>murder-furries</i>, even if <i>you</i> didn't knew of them, your players may now and have expectations you won't deliver. And they may be important to them. That's all before we even touch on how they may take the foes they'll face, or the reasons for facing them.<br /> <br /> So that's the conundrum of the info, the most important one, how you explain things <i>before the very game</i> begins, so you avoid as much as possible the hard situation of dealing with player frustration, not pontual frustration, but overall frustration with the game as a whole.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555769#post1555769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">5 - <b>Deal with actual player knowledge and understanding.</b> How complex or deep could it possibly be?</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem here is that you can't actually control for this.<br /> <br /> You can't be sure your players are brand new to the game, only that they claim it. They may lie, they may misremember, they may misunderstand something. They may never have played, but have read a lot about it.<br /> <br /> Likewise, you can't control for the cultural baggage of your players. Just because you thought that it doesn't means everyone will, there are lots of complex stories out there and players may piece things together from many places, in many ways, from movies they watched, books they read, legends they heard, things they saw on the internet. They may make incorrect assumptions that will inform them in the wrong direction and lead them astray from the plot, as it may result in some quite precise conclusions that may spoil the intended effect if you don't consider this in advance.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555769#post1555769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">6 - Finally, you have to deal with both<b> players that pretend to not know the game, but actually know it, and players that do research about it between sessions and may end up knowing even more than you do</b>.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Players don't always do what you ask them. That's why I said this is easier to control with a group you already know well.<br /> <br /> The internet exists and there's a lot of information about the setting out there. And here, by the way. A curious player may search for anything about the game and start learning things they don't know you don't want them to know.<br /> <br /> Despite it having more than a few problems, there are two easy access wikias about WtA with lots and lots of information about the setting and what the Garou can do. The books are easy to buy and easy to get without buying. There are discussions galore in forums like this one and many others, as in Twitter and Reddit.<br /> <br /> How do you control your players' access to life?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1555708-designing-a-chronicle-to-introduce-new-players-into-werewolf-the-apocalypse?p=1555777#post1555777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, I'm highly advocating for a &quot;introducing a bunch of new players to WtA&quot; not start with &quot;a bunch of freshly changed Garou that have no clue&quot; that climaxes with their first Moot. As you seem to agree, that means a lot of learning but outside of the context where doing is a primary consideration. I'm advocating starting with closer to base-line starting PCs and a Moot because it's a lot of doing in a highly controlled event in-universe to create a framework for the players to feel comfortable in while they learn about stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to second that.<br /> <br /> The reason I pointed all that isn't just because I want to help (although I want), but to make it clear that this is a very tall order.<br /> <br /> My experience with such projects is that they're actually good for experienced players that want to play through the growing pains of a cub. And you can bet that their OoC knowledge gets in the way, but at least it works.<br /> <br /> Having this kind of experimental play with new players seems fun at first, but is actually hard as fuck, and too many things can go wrong. At the very least I would suggest experienced RPG players that are new just to the WoD, but have at least some years roleplaying under their belt, so they know what to expect of this kind of experience.<br /> <br /> By the way, I was excited when I saw the title of the thread precisely because I thought I would find actual advice on doing this that could be useful. This is not a conundrum I've cracked already, and I have almost 3 decades of experience with RPGs, 20 years STing WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-07T16:32:40-08:00","id":1556320,"title":"","content":"I have given this a lot of thought, but now I think I know where to begin:<br /> <br /> <u><b>Let's Open the Chronicle with a Moot.</b></u> The key here would be in letting the older players lead the new players, and everyone having fun with teaching/learning the mechanics.<br /> <br /> Veteran players can have fun teaching new players the ropes (which then builds party cohesion between the players, and pack cohesion with the characters, simultaneously and organically).<br /> This also allows the older players to be in control over the pacing so it feels more like organic character interaction and <b>player-led mentorship</b> rather than &quot;We have players who never played werewolf, so this is our mandatory tutorial.&quot;<br /> <br /> Plus, this level of connection between characters and players being in the same boat as each other, feels unique because the older players' characters are the ones who can get a lot of fun out of helping the new players get comfortable in the skin of their characters.<br /> <br /> The player choices are about getting familiarized with game mechanics in a fun, in-universe way (again, getting the &quot;wait, WE CAN DO THAT?!&quot; reaction out of new players as they learn things).<br /> <br /> <br /> Having a Story (roughly 1-3 game sessions) dedicated to this, instead of a whole Chronicle is probably the smartest option here, and I was biting off way more than I could chew. NOT A WHOLE CHRONICLE, but definitely a good start to a chronicle, where a primary mood could be about learning(?)<br /> <br /> <br /> <sub>(Random side-note: In order to think outside the box, there needs to be a box. Player choices during character creation are a huge part of that box's construction, and I am learning as I brainstorm this.\u200b)</sub>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}